TOPIC 2 : FUNDAMENTALS OF A COMPUTER OPERATIONS
Intro: Computers work by following instructions really fast, and understanding the CPU and its parts helps know how tasks are done
CPU – Central Processing Unit: the brain of the computer, does all the thinking and work.
ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit): does maths and logic like adding numbers or comparing values. e.g., calculates totals in Excel or checks answers in a quiz app.
Control Unit: directs data flow between CPU, memory, n devices. e.g., tells the printer when to print or loads the next level in a game.
Registers: small, super-fast memory inside CPU for temp data. e.g., storing a number b4 adding it to another.
System Clock: keeps operations in sync, sets pace of processing. Higher clock speed = programs run faster.
Machine Cycle: CPU works in steps: fetch → decode → execute → store. e.g., typing in Excel goes through these steps automatically.
Parallel Processing: using more than one core or processor at same time to finish tasks faster. e.g., running browser, Excel, n a game at same time without slowing down.
Performance: depends on CPU speed, number of cores, n memory size. affects how fast tasks like analyzing big datasets in Excel or Python are completed.
