TOPIC4:Computer Hardware II – Storage Devices & Memory
Intro: Computers need memory and storage to keep info temporarily or permanently. Understanding the types helps manage big data.
Primary Memory: fast, temporary storage used while the computer is running. e.g., RAM stores data while you work on Excel, ROM holds basic instructions for starting the computer.
Secondary Memory: permanent storage for files and programs. e.g., HDDs, SSDs, or cloud storage like Google Drive.
Cache Memory: very small, super-fast memory near the CPU to store frequently used data. e.g., helps Excel calculations run faster without fetching from RAM each time.
RAID: combining multiple drives for speed or backup. e.g., a lab storing lots of experiment data safely.
Disk Structure: disks are divided into tracks, sectors, clusters, and surfaces, which affects read/write speed. e.g., reading a large dataset is faster if data is organized well.
Big Data Implications: organizing memory and storage matters for large datasets, like biostatistics or financial info. e.g., using SSD instead of HDD speeds up data retrieval.
Memory Unit – Note
Memory Unit: part of the computer that stores data and instructions temporarily or permanently so the CPU can use them. Basically, it’s where the computer “remembers” stuff.
PRIMARY MEMORY V SECONDARY MEMORY
Primary Memory: fast, temporary memory used by the CPU while programs are running.
Examples: RAM – stores files or programs you’re using now; lost when the computer is off. ROM – stores instructions for starting the computer; permanent.
Key points: fast, small, temporary, directly accessed by CPU.
Secondary Memory: permanent storage for files, programs, and data.
Examples: HDD, SSD, USB drives, cloud storage.
Key points: slower than primary, can hold large amounts of data, not lost when computer is off.
NB: primary = working memory (used now), secondary = long-term storage (kept forever).



