TOPIC6:DATA FILES AND DATA FILE MANAGEMENT
Intro: Computers store data in files. Learning how to manage files is important for organizing and retrieving information efficiently.


Types of Files:
Random Files: can access any record directly without reading others. e.g., database tables where you can go to any row instantly.
Sequential Files: data must be read in order. e.g., old log files or text files where you read line by line.
Structured Files: organized in a fixed format. e.g., Excel spreadsheets or CSV files.
Unstructured Files: no fixed format, harder to search. e.g., videos, images, or Word documents.
File Creation & Management: involves making files, organizing them in folders, and naming them properly. e.g., saving lab data in separate folders by date or experiment.
Indexing & Retrieval: helps locate data faster. e.g., using an index in a database to find a student’s record quickly.
Optimization: arranging files and folders for better performance. e.g., keeping large datasets on SSD for faster access.
Relational Databases: store data in tables connected by relationships. e.g., student info table linked to grades table in Access or SQL.
Normalization: organizing database tables to reduce duplication and improve efficiency. e.g., separating student contact info from grades to avoid repeating the same data.
Practical Tip: creating, managing, and querying datasets in Access or SQL helps you handle large amounts of data efficiently. e.g., filtering exam results or checking for missing entries.


